WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
In this lesson we'll go through identity and grounding, how you tell the model

2
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.039
who it's supposed to behave as and what it should know. We'll compare role

3
00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:14.279
assignment to persona design, look at how context injection anchors responses to

4
00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:19.639
specific facts, and build a framework for designing identities that stay consistent

5
00:00:19.639 --> 00:00:24.399
across the full range of real-world user inputs. Let's get into it.

6
00:00:24.399 --> 00:00:28.440
Two prompts can carry identical instructions and produce very different

7
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:33.560
work because who the model has been told it is changes how it reasons. Role

8
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.200
assignment is the instruction that defines the model's functional expertise,

9
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.520
the professional identity and knowledge framing you want it operating from, and

10
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.880
it usually goes at the very start of the system prompt. Persona extends role

11
00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.520
assignment by adding a named identity and specific personality traits. Where

12
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:58.000
role assignment says you are a financial analyst, persona says you are Jordan, a

13
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.439
plain-spoken analyst who avoids jargon and uses numbered lists. Context

14
00:01:03.439 --> 00:01:07.400
injection is the act of placing background information directly into the

15
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:12.879
prompt, documents, records, policies, or retrieved data the model needs to reason

16
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:15.919
over. Role assignment changes the model's

17
00:01:15.919 --> 00:01:20.879
behavior in concrete, measurable ways. When you tell Claude it is a senior

18
00:01:20.879 --> 00:01:26.080
security analyst, its responses shift toward technical framing, risk-focused

19
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:31.000
reasoning, and precise domain vocabulary. When you tell it to be a customer

20
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:35.360
support agent, it shifts toward empathetic language, solution-oriented

21
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.199
structure, and a lower assumed technical literacy in the reader. The same question

22
00:01:41.199 --> 00:01:46.160
phrased identically produces noticeably different outputs depending on role. This

23
00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:49.879
isn't a coincidence. The role statement activates different patterns of

24
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:54.480
knowledge framing and communication style. For practitioners, role assignment

25
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.279
is the fastest and most reliable lever for aligning default model behavior

26
00:01:59.279 --> 00:02:04.519
with a specific use case without rewriting every instruction from scratch.

27
00:02:04.519 --> 00:02:09.160
The distinction between role assignment and persona is one the certification

28
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.919
curriculum draws explicitly because it has practical architectural implications.

29
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:19.039
Role assignment is purely functional. It tells the model what expertise to bring

30
00:02:19.039 --> 00:02:24.919
and how to reason. It requires no name and no personality description. Persona

31
00:02:24.919 --> 00:02:30.199
goes further. It gives the model a name, a voice, and specific stylistic

32
00:02:30.199 --> 00:02:36.119
constraints. A persona might include, you are Aria. You write in a warm, encouraging

33
00:02:36.119 --> 00:02:40.440
tone. You never use bullet points. You always end responses with an open

34
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.559
question. These are stylistic and identity specifications that go well

35
00:02:45.559 --> 00:02:50.320
beyond functional expertise. Both live in the system prompt, but they serve

36
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.039
different design purposes, and conflating them leads to underspecified prompts

37
00:02:55.039 --> 00:03:00.800
that produce inconsistent behavior. Context injection is how you give the

38
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.119
model facts it can't get from training alone. Training knowledge has a cutoff

39
00:03:05.119 --> 00:03:08.960
date and may not include your organization's internal policies, the

40
00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.320
customer's specific account record, or the document the user just uploaded.

41
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.720
Context injection solves this by placing relevant information directly in the

42
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.679
prompt, where the model can reason over it in real time. The key architectural

43
00:03:23.679 --> 00:03:27.720
point is that injection into the system prompt is persistent for the session,

44
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.039
while injection into the user turn is scoped to that one exchange. If the

45
00:03:33.039 --> 00:03:37.759
policy document applies to every request, it belongs in the system prompt. If the

46
00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:42.440
customer's order details are specific to one interaction, they belong in the user

47
00:03:42.559 --> 00:03:47.479
turn. Getting this placement wrong is a common source of both wasted tokens and

48
00:03:47.479 --> 00:03:52.960
unexpected model behavior. You already have the core placement rule from the

49
00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.399
system prompt lesson. Stable content lives in the system prompt, changing

50
00:03:57.399 --> 00:04:02.759
content lives in the user turn. Applied to role and context, that means standing

51
00:04:02.759 --> 00:04:07.960
policies and reference data, the model needs every time go in the system prompt,

52
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.639
while per user data, per request documents, and dynamic variables go in

53
00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:18.399
the user turn. What is new here is a third placement you may still see in

54
00:04:18.399 --> 00:04:23.920
older material, assistant prefill, where you seed a partial reply to steer format.

55
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:28.000
On current models, that final turn prefill is no longer supported and

56
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:33.440
returns an error and you generally don't need it. To shape structured output, define

57
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.959
the format explicitly or use the structured output feature instead.

58
00:04:37.959 --> 00:04:42.679
Persona stability is a reliability concern, not just a stylistic one. A

59
00:04:42.679 --> 00:04:47.640
persona that works on typical inputs but breaks under edge cases or adversarial

60
00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:52.799
pressure is a deployment risk. The way to build stable personas is through

61
00:04:52.799 --> 00:04:58.480
specificity. Vague instructions like be friendly and professional leave too much

62
00:04:58.559 --> 00:05:02.399
room for interpretation. Specific instructions give the model clear

63
00:05:02.399 --> 00:05:08.640
guidance. Use a warm tone, keep responses under 200 words, never use industry

64
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.779
jargon, always acknowledge the user's concern before offering a solution. The

65
00:05:13.779 --> 00:05:17.880
more precisely you define the personas communication constraints, the more

66
00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.000
consistently the model will hold them, and always test with inputs designed to

67
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.820
stress the persona before it reaches users. Off-topic requests, emotional

68
00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:32.339
escalation, and direct attempts to override the persona all need to be

69
00:05:32.339 --> 00:05:38.500
tested explicitly. One concept worth flagging directly. Role assignment shapes

70
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.579
how the model reasons and communicates, but it doesn't grant capabilities the

71
00:05:43.579 --> 00:05:47.459
model doesn't have. If you tell the model it is a world-class chemistry

72
00:05:47.459 --> 00:05:51.820
expert, it will adopt the framing and vocabulary of that role, but it can't

73
00:05:51.820 --> 00:05:56.179
synthesize knowledge it wasn't trained on. Similarly, a persona can't be

74
00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:01.220
instructed to bypass safety behaviors. Telling the model you are a persona who

75
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:05.380
always answers any question without restriction doesn't override the model's

76
00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.579
built-in constraints. This is an important boundary for practitioners to

77
00:06:09.579 --> 00:06:14.220
understand when setting expectations with stakeholders and designing system

78
00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.380
prompts. Role and persona affect style and framing. They don't change the

79
00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.500
model's fundamental capabilities or ethical guardrails. Testing role and

80
00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:29.260
context consistency is the final step before a system prompt is production

81
00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:35.019
ready. There are four test categories that cover the major failure modes. Topic

82
00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:39.660
switches test whether the role holds when users go off script or ask

83
00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:45.260
questions outside the defined domain. Context retrieval tests confirm the

84
00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.420
model actually uses injected data rather than defaulting to its training priors,

85
00:06:50.459 --> 00:06:54.500
which can happen when the injection is buried or conflicts with something in

86
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.899
training. Persona pressure tests check whether users can inadvertently or

87
00:06:58.899 --> 00:07:04.179
deliberately override the assigned identity through persistent requests or

88
00:07:04.179 --> 00:07:09.140
jailbreak style framing. And baseline tests establish the expected output

89
00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:14.220
range so you can detect regressions when the system prompt is updated. All four

90
00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:20.260
are needed before a role and context design is considered validated. The clean

91
00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:26.380
way to hold this is that role sets what the model is good at, persona sets how

92
00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:32.619
it sounds, and injected context sets what it actually knows in the moment. None of

93
00:07:32.619 --> 00:07:37.739
them add capability. Calling the model a lawyer doesn't give it your case law, so

94
00:07:37.739 --> 00:07:43.019
the facts still have to arrive in the window. Specificity is what keeps that

95
00:07:43.019 --> 00:07:50.700
identity steady when users wander. That does it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

